Approach 1:(Sorting Approach)

class Solution {
    public int missingNumber(int[] nums) {
       
        int i=0;
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        while(i<=nums.length)
        {
            if( i==nums.length|| nums[i]!=i )
            {
                return i;
            }
            i++;
        }
        return 0;
    }
}

Complexity:
Time = O(n logn)
space = O(1)

Approach 2(Sum formula)

class Solution {
    public int missingNumber(int[] nums) {
        int n=nums.length;
        int expected_sum=n*(n+1)/2;
        int actual_sum=0;
        for(int i=0;i<n;i++)
        {
            actual_sum+=nums[i];
        }
        return expected_sum-actual_sum;
    }
}
Complexity:
Time = O(n)
Space = (1)

Approach 3(XOR)
class Solution {
    public int missingNumber(int[] nums) {
        int n=nums.length;
        int xor=nums.length;
        for(int i=0;i<n;i++)
        {
            xor=xor^i^nums[i];
        }
        return xor;
    }
}

Complexity:
Time = O(n}
Space = O(1)

XOR is taken between the expected array and original array(the elements in original array cancel out the elements in expected array so the extra elements in the expected array is the missing element) 
